small model agents idea
i am looking at solving a problem where we have data which is limited to my domain say travel or finance and i want to create a SLM by training a large model like LLM and get a docker container out which has my slm and only works on my model nothing else .. and then add a pipeline which can keep feeding my slm wiht more daily/hourly data and get answers with an agent using my slm container

Answer: 


Nice — here’s a concise, practical plan you can use to build a Small/Single-domain Language Model (SLM) packaged as a self-contained Docker container and expose it to an agent-driven pipeline that continuously updates the model with new domain data (travel or finance).

High-level goals

Train or fine-tune a compact domain model (SLM) that reliably answers only within your domain.
Package the SLM in a secure, auditable Docker container that exposes a simple inference API.
Build an “agent” that queries the SLM, orchestrates pipelines (pre/post processing, retrieval, retrieval-augmented-generation), and handles streaming updates.
Create an automated data pipeline to ingest, validate, and (optionally) fine-tune or index fresh domain data on an hourly/daily cadence.
Architecture overview (components)

Data layer

Sources: transactional systems, logs, bookings, prices, market feeds, support tickets, knowledge bases, CSVs, APIs.
Ingestion: ETL jobs (Airflow, Prefect, Dagster) or lightweight cron + scripts.
Validation & normalization: schema checks, type validation, deduplication, canonicalization (normalizing currency, date/time, location names).
Storage: object store (S3), time-series DB for streaming metrics, and a metadata catalog.
Retrieval / Vector DB (optional but recommended)

Tooling: FAISS, Milvus, Pinecone, Weaviate.
Create embeddings for documents/utterances to support retrieval-augmented responses (RAG).
Periodically upsert new embeddings as new data arrives.
Model (SLM)

Options:
Fine-tune a compact LLM (e.g., Llama-2-7B or smaller distilled models) on domain-specific corpora.
Train a domain adapter or LoRA on a base model so the container only serves the adapter + base weights if licensing allows.
Alternatively, use a retrieval-only approach with a small generative head.
Constrain model to domain via:
Training data selection (only domain data).
Prompt templates and system instructions that insist on domain-only responses.
Safety filters / refusal policies for out-of-domain queries.
Inference service (Docker)

Expose a small HTTP/gRPC REST server (FastAPI, Flask, or Go) that:
Accepts queries and returns JSON answers + provenance (sources used).
Limits functionality to domain tasks; enforces policies (no external web browsing).
Optionally supports streaming responses (server-sent events or HTTP chunking).
Include health checks, logging, metrics (Prometheus).
Container hardening: minimal base image, non-root user, limit network egress (firewall/iptables), disable package managers inside container.
Agent layer

Purpose: orchestrate work around the SLM:
Decide whether to call SLM directly or use retrieval first.
Route queries to specialized micro-agents (e.g., pricing agent, booking agent, risk-check agent).
Handle multi-step workflows (retrieve->predict->validate->respond; or call external APIs).
Implement with a lightweight agent framework or custom code:
Use a planner + executor pattern: planner breaks down tasks; executor calls SLM & retrieval services.
Keep agent logic deterministic and auditable — log each step and decision.
Guardrails: require SLM to return citations, confidence scores, and refusal messages for unknowns.
Update pipeline (continuous learning / index updates)

For retrieval data (RAG):
New documents -> embedding -> upsert to vector DB (near real-time or batch).
For model updates:
Collect high-quality labeled examples (user feedback, human review).
Maintain a training dataset with versioning (DVC, Git LFS, or datasets repo).
Periodic fine-tuning pipeline (scheduled retrains, or incremental LoRA updates).
Validate new model on a holdout set and human-in-the-loop tests before promoting.
Canary rollouts: run new model in shadow mode or a subset of traffic.
Monitoring, auditing & governance

Track model drift, accuracy (via labeled checks), latency, and throughput.
Store request/response logs and provenance for audits and compliance.
Add rate limits, authentication (mTLS, API keys), RBAC for who can query or update.
Concrete implementation roadmap (12–16 weeks, example)

Week 1–2: Data inventory, schema design, and minimal ingestion pipeline.
Week 3–4: Set up retrieval store and create embeddings for seed docs.
Week 5–7: Fine-tune or LoRA a compact model on domain data; build inference API around it.
Week 8: Containerize SLM with hardened Dockerfile + minimal runtime and tests.
Week 9–10: Build agent orchestration layer with planner/executor and RAG integration.
Week 11: Add CI/CD for model builds and container image builds; add monitoring.
Week 12: Implement update pipeline for embeddings + scheduled fine-tuning; canary rollout.
Week 13–16: Implement feedback loop, labeling UI, safety tests, and production hardening.
Example stack (tools)

Orchestration: Airflow / Prefect / Dagster
Model training: Hugging Face Transformers + Accelerate, BitsAndBytes for 8-bit, LoRA (peft)
Embeddings: OpenAI embeddings or local embedding model (e.g., all-MiniLM)
Vector DB: Milvus, FAISS, Pinecone, or Weaviate
Inference server: FastAPI + Uvicorn or Triton Inference Server (for GPU)
Container: Docker (distroless or slim base), k8s for orchestration in prod
Monitoring: Prometheus + Grafana, Sentry for errors
CI/CD: GitHub Actions or GitLab CI
Security: Vault for secrets, OPA for policy checks
Design/implementation tips

Keep the container stateless. Store persistent data (indexes, models) in external volumes or S3.
Use small base models so your container image + RAM footprint is manageable on typical infra.
For domain-only enforcement, combine dataset curation, system prompt constraints, and a lightweight classifier that rejects OOD queries before hitting the model.
Prefer LoRA/adapters if you want quick, low-resource updates that keep base model frozen.
Ensure traceability: attach document source IDs and timestamps to any answer that used external knowledge.
Maintain separate dev/qa/prod images and sign images (image digest) before deployment.
Security & compliance considerations

Restrict container network egress so the SLM can’t access arbitrary endpoints.
Use strict API authentication and role-based access control.
Log data access with PII masking; encrypt logs at rest.
If storing financial/travel PII, follow relevant regulations (PCI, GDPR).
Keep training data provenance so you can remove user data on request.
Minimal example: single-repo layout

/data_ingest/ (scripts + DAGs)
/embeddings/ (embed + vector upsert)
/model_training/ (training scripts, LoRA adapters)
/inference/ (FastAPI server, Dockerfile)
/agent/ (planner, router, task executors)
/ci/ (pipeline definitions)
/infra/ (k8s manifests, helm chart)
